  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nOn Wednesday, the models show the moist axis retrograding westward\nslightly into the Interstate 35 corridor and portions of the Hill\nCountry. With strong afternoon heating (highs upper 80s to upper\n90s), we should see another round of isolated to scattered\nconvection develop from the coastal plains westward into the Hill\nCountry. Late in the day, the focus for convection will shift\nfarther west into western Val Verde County where southwest flow\naloft and enhanced moisture from 'Simon' will slowly nudge\neastward. The WPC Day 3 outlook highlights the western half of Val\nVerde County in a Level 1 of 4 risk for heavy rainfall. Current\ntrends suggest the better chance for rain would likely occur very\nlate Wednesday or early Thursday morning. Enhanced moisture and\nlift remain focused over our western areas again on Thursday into\nFriday, with WPC showing a level 2 of 4 risk for heavy rain across\nVal Verde into western Edwards and Kinney counties, with a level 1\nof 4 risk into the Rio Grande plains northward into the western\nHill Country. \n\nThe next feature of interest will be a cold front set to move into\nthe region late Thursday into early Friday morning. Models\ncontinue to show higher rain chances across portions of the\nsouthern Edwards where the front will encounter high moisture\nlevels, with much lower or non-existent rain chances for areas\nalong and east of I-35. It will be windy behind the front on\nFriday, followed by much cooler temperatures and dry weather for\nthe upcoming weekend. Weekend highs will be in the lower 70s to\nlower for most of the region along with lows in the lower 50s to\nlower 60s. Some low rain chances may enter the forecast on Monday\nwith a broad upper low to our west. However, chances should remain\nlow.",
